1. d4 e6 2. e4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e5 Nfd7 6. Bxe7 Qxe7 7. f4 O-O 8.
Nf3 f6 (8... c5 {is usually played first.}) 9. Bd3 c5 10. h4 ({Hans Niemann -
who he? - preferred} 10. O-O {and beat Mujumdar in 25 moves in the 2019 World
Open. Other moves are possible.}) 10... cxd4 (10... Rd8 {was played in
Chajes-Levenfish, Carlsbad 1911.}) 11. Bxh7+ $5 {Engines are unconvinced but
it is crying out to be played, isn't it?} Kxh7 12. Ng5+ Kg8 $2 ({Here Black
should perhaps play} 12... Kh6 {, accompanied by a quizzical look at the
opponent, questioning how he intends to continue the attack. A piece in the
hand is worth two in the bush.}) 13. Qh5 fxg5 14. hxg5 g6 {Obligatory.} 15.
Qh8+ Kf7 16. Rh7+ Ke8 17. Rxe7+ Kxe7 18. Qh7+ Rf7 19. Nxd5+ exd5 20. Qxg6 {
[diag] Black has secured a rook and three minor pieces for his queen and three
pawns, which he might reasonably have imagined was handsome compensation, but
positionally he still has problems to solve.} Nb6 $2 ({After} 20... Nf8 $1 {
Black might have been better, but the text is a serious error.}) 21. Qd6+ Ke8
22. e6 $5 Bxe6 23. O-O-O $1 {The rook quickly joins forces with the queen and
Black hasn't time to organise a defence.} Re7 24. Rh1 Bg8 ({It's too difficult
to defend, at least for a human. Stockfish finds} 24... N8d7 25. Rh8+ Nf8 26.
g6 Bg8 $3 {when} 27. Rxg8 $2 {allows} Re1+ 28. Kd2 Nc4+ {, etc.}) 25. b3 $2
N8d7 $2 ({After} 25... Re6 $1 {Black is back in the game.}) 26. Rh8 Kf8 27.
Rxg8+ Kxg8 28. Qxe7 Nf8 29. f5 {Source: Rosselson scoresheet. Hampstead 12½,
Combined Universities 7½.} 1-0

